nodecode-minimax-video is a rewrite of rriggs's rriggs/hermes-plugin-minimax-video:

  https://github.com/rriggs/hermes-plugin-minimax-video
  commit 759bec0f124c50e868facc8da5e6cf054403d761
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Colorado Rob (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's whole video surface, as one Nodecode cell. The three
modes and how a call is routed to one (an image is a first frame, reference
images are a subject, neither is a prompt alone), the models and their
per-mode resolution defaults (768P on the Hailuo models, 720P on the older
ones), the 6-or-10-second clamp, the asynchronous task flow (submit to
/v1/video_generation, poll /v1/query/video_generation, retrieve the file from
/v1/files/retrieve, then download it), the local-image-to-base64-data-URI
path with its 10 MB cap, the answer's fields (file, bytes, model, mode,
duration, resolution, task id, file id) and the error text with the setting
that fixes it. Two things are added because a tool call outlives no render:
video_status takes a task id a call answered with, and /video is the same
call from the operator's side with flags.

Left out: the Hermes video_gen provider interface itself (list_models with
its four display rows, capabilities, get_setup_schema and the is_available
probe), because a Nodecode cell is not a provider lane and its settings panel
is its own section declaration; the plugin's own polling constants, now the
section's wait_seconds and poll_seconds; and the 720p default the tool layer
always passed, which the cell reads as "the call said nothing" and so lets
the model's own default stand. The requested aspect ratio, which MiniMax
ignores on the wire and which the original only echoed back in its answer, is
not carried. The Python requests library is not here either: nlk:http speaks
the three endpoints directly.
